{ import: "contract.eu"
  doc: "SV3 eu-u9xj.1 - validate forces only the paths the spec names.

Cost paid only where written, taken literally. Each check below would raise
or diverge if the interpreter forced a subtree the spec does not name, so a
regression in the forcing discipline turns this file from PASS into a hard
error - which the harness reports as a failure.

The bomb blocks are built with block(...) rather than a block literal: demand
analysis makes a literal block's field values effectively strict, so a literal
would detonate at construction time and gate nothing." }

` :suppress
bomb-blk: block([[:a, 1], [:b, panic("SV3: an unmentioned subtree was forced")]])

# A record spec with no fields enumerates the spine and forces no value.
t1: (bomb-blk validate(s"{ .. }")) nil?

# An open spec naming only `a` forces exactly `a`.
t2: (bomb-blk validate(s"{a: number, ..}")) nil?

# A CLOSED spec still only enumerates keys - closedness is a key-set
# comparison, never a value forcing. `b` is surplus, so one :unexpected entry
# is reported and its `found` is the key symbol, not the value.
` :suppress
closed-r: bomb-blk validate(s"{a: number}")

t3: (closed-r count) = 1
t4: (closed-r first).kind = :unexpected
t5: (closed-r first).found = [:b]

# `any` and `top` accept without forcing at all.
t6: (bomb-blk validate(s"{a: any, b: any}")) nil?
t7: (bomb-blk validate(s"{a: top, b: top}")) nil?

# A type variable is unconstrained at runtime and forces nothing.
t8: (bomb-blk validate(s"{a: number, b: r}")) nil?

# An unrecognised tag inside a well-formed spec accepts without forcing -
# parity with as-spec's any? fallthrough.
t9: (bomb-blk validate([:t-record,
                        { a: [:t-field, :required, [:t-prim, :number]]
                          b: [:t-field, :required, [:t-mu, :X, [:t-prim, :number]]] },
                        false])) nil?

# An infinite list under an unnamed key: its WHNF is cheap, its spine is not.
# A spec that does not name it must not walk the spine.
` :suppress
inf-blk: block([[:a, 1], [:b, ints-from(1)]])

t10: (inf-blk validate(s"{a: number, ..}")) nil?
t11: (inf-blk validate(s"{ .. }")) nil?

# Nested: the bomb is two levels down and still unnamed.
` :suppress
nested-bomb: block([[:outer, block([[:x, 1], [:y, panic("SV3: nested subtree was forced")]])]])

t12: (nested-bomb validate(s"{outer: {x: number, ..}, ..}")) nil?

# ensure inherits the same discipline and returns the (still partly unforced)
# value.
t13: (bomb-blk ensure-name) = "a"

ensure-name(b): ((b validate(s"{a: number, ..}")) nil?) then("a", "forced")

RESULT: if([t1, t2, t3, t4, t5, t6, t7, t8, t9, t10, t11, t12, t13] all-true?,
           :PASS, :FAIL)
